\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A negative paired factor}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Let a generator of #{\mathbb Z/3} rotate an oriented Euclidean plane through #{2\pi /3}. Its complexification has just the two conjugate nonreal characters. Choose the representative whose complex structure gives the standard oriented area #{\omega}. The two alternating forms #{\omega} and #{-\omega} are invariant under the same rotation. Their [Pfaffians](fdip-008I) in that same unit volume are #{1} and #{-1}, respectively, since the top power in dimension two is the form itself. Thus a pair of nonreal labels can contribute a negative sign. Replacing the volume by #{2\omega} keeps its orientation while halving each scalar.}
